WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.580
Zombie doctors, cranky gods, and your friendly&nbsp;
neighborhood swamp monster were just some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:11.280
the surprises the MCU threw at us in 2022.&nbsp;
And did you catch the latest mutant reveal?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.440
"Moon Knight" was, by and large, Oscar&nbsp;
Isaac's show. The accomplished actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.440
who's as comfortable in blockbusters like&nbsp;
"Star Wars" as he is in intimate dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.080
is a fitting choice to play a superhero with&nbsp;
a dissociative identity disorder. One of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.780
alter egos is Steven Grant, a meek museum&nbsp;
gift shop employee. Another is Marc Spector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:35.100
a confident mercenary who is the&nbsp;
avatar of the Egyptian god Khonshu.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.940
"Hello, man in the mirror. I was&nbsp;
wondering if you'd pop up again."

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.960
"I know you're scared."

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.134
"A bit, yeah."

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.840
Steven meets Layla El-Faouly, an&nbsp;
Indiana Jones type archeologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.460
who is also Spector's estranged wife. She's&nbsp;
upset that her husband has abandoned her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.180
and Steven can't believe he'd ever screw&nbsp;
things up with such an incredible woman.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.860
Layla spends most of the series with an&nbsp;
alternately awestruck, scared, and confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.960
Steven. It's fun to watch her as she and this&nbsp;
alter ego of her partner in love and archaeology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.660
uncover tombs and escape henchmen. Still, actress&nbsp;
May Calamawy never has as much to do as Isaac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.200
and there isn't much development of her&nbsp;
character that hints at a last-act twist.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.100
However, in the finale, Layla&nbsp;
becomes the avatar of Taweret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.240
the Egyptian hippo goddess, in an attempt&nbsp;
to stop Harrow and the god of judgment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.760
Ammit. Her transformation into the MCU's version&nbsp;
of the Marvel Comics character Scarlet Scarab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.801
complete with metallic wings extended, makes for&nbsp;
one of the most exciting moments in "Moon Knight."

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.840
"Moon Knight" proves that the MCU likes to&nbsp;
keep us guessing until the very end. Fans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.920
the "Moon Knight" comics always knew that Steven&nbsp;
and Marc shared their body with a third identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:46.320
Jake Lockley, who was often portrayed as a cab&nbsp;
driver with a busted nose. Readers anticipated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.180
Oscar Isaac might get the chance to play this&nbsp;
grittier alter ego, and the show was peppered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.560
with subtle clues throughout. For example,&nbsp;
whenever Moon Knight resorted to extreme violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.840
the audience rarely saw it, and Steven&nbsp;
simply came to, disoriented. Most fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.980
assumed it was Marc causing the bloodshed,&nbsp;
but some suspected it was actually Jake.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.600
In a trippy sequence at the end of Episode&nbsp;
4, Marc and Steven are trapped in a surreal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.460
psychiatric hospital and pass by a locked&nbsp;
sarcophagus, which leads to speculation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.260
that Jake is trapped inside. The season finale&nbsp;
does not actually confirm Lockley's existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.840
but in true MCU style, a "mid-credits" scene&nbsp;
leaves no doubt. A man wearing black leather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.940
driving gloves wheels Harrow out to a limo and&nbsp;
rudely dumps him inside. The driver then kicks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.020
the wheelchair aside as Harrow confronts&nbsp;
Khonshu, who has been waiting in the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.940
seat. We then learn from the Moon god himself that&nbsp;
Marc and Steven are both off the hook for all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:44.243
previous murderous mayhem because Lockley had been&nbsp;
there all along, happily doing the god's bidding.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.680
Rumors were running wild throughout fandom that&nbsp;
major celebrities and some as-of-yet-unseen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.840
characters might make their MCU debuts in "Doctor&nbsp;
Strange in the Multiverse of Madness."A trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.860
for the film featuring Patrick Stewart's&nbsp;
voice only added to the speculation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.420
Professor X and the Illuminaticould appear in the&nbsp;
sequel. Sure enough, partway through the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.700
there was Charles Xavier, seated next to&nbsp;
Black Bolt, Captain Carter, Karl Mordo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.780
Captain Marvel, and Reed Richards.&nbsp;
While it did not come as much of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.200
shock that Earth-838's Illuminati&nbsp;
made an appearance in "Multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.820
of Madness," what threw fans for a loop was&nbsp;
how director Sam Rami chose to deploy them.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:26.220
The X-Men and The Fantastic Four have been&nbsp;
eagerly anticipated additions to the MCU, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.080
filmmakers totally pulled the rug out from under&nbsp;
audiences with Wanda Maximoff in complete and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.480
terrifying villain mode, killing off each member&nbsp;
of the Illuminati in creative and disgusting ways.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.550
"Haven't you had enough?"

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.654
"I could do this all day."

00:03:40.654 --> 00:03:45.420
Raimi and scriptwriter Michael Waldron's&nbsp;
devilishly hilarious bait-and-switch gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.320
fans what they thought they wanted, then&nbsp;
took it all away in spectacular fashion.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.340
With Sam Raimi, the man behind "The&nbsp;
Evil Dead" in the director's chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.980
fans expected the MCU to head into some groovy new&nbsp;
directions, but they couldn't have guessed Stephen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.540
Strange and his evil doppelganger would&nbsp;
literally fight to the death with musical notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.580
in one of Marvel's most inventive battles yet.&nbsp;
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.900
full of Raimi-style flourishes that are as&nbsp;
playful as they are gruesome. From a giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:17.220
eyeball getting impaled with a street lamp to&nbsp;
Wanda's chilling jump scare, the movie doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:22.701
pull any punches. Even Bruce Campbell, as "Pizza&nbsp;
Poppa," is doomed to punch himself repeatedly.

00:04:23.798 --> 00:04:25.298
"It's over!"

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:30.780
All the madness comes to a crescendo in a dark&nbsp;
and daring final act. To defeat Wanda and save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.760
America Chavez, Strange 616 dream walks&nbsp;
into Defender Strange's decaying corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.380
conveniently stashed in a rooftop shed in&nbsp;
his universe. The souls of the damned arrive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.160
to stop him since possessing dead bodies&nbsp;
is a major no-no, but he wins them over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:49.020
A demon-enhanced zombie Strange, complete with&nbsp;
broken fingers and chunks of his face missing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.220
holds off Wanda long enough for America&nbsp;
to show her the error of her ways.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.340
"This time it's gonna take more&nbsp;
than killing me to kill me."

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.360
The MCU's second television series of the&nbsp;
year was responsible for some notable firsts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.720
not the least of which is that "Ms.&nbsp;
Marvel" is centered around the studio's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.520
first Muslim superhero. Kamala Khan who&nbsp;
is a 16-year-old Pakistani American living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.760
in a Jersey City apartment with&nbsp;
her supportive yet strict family.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.440
When the series begins, all she wants is&nbsp;
permission to attend AvengerCon and cosplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.140
as her idol, Captain Marvel. However, her folks&nbsp;
disapprove, so she sneaks out. Her best friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:29.160
and one of many potential love interests,&nbsp;
Bruno, makes her a pair of gloves for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.200
costume contest, but she forgets them in the&nbsp;
bathroom. Instead, she throws on a mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.880
heirloom bangle bracelet which appears to&nbsp;
turn her into a superhero in her own right.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.080
Six episodes later, when it seems that the&nbsp;
Department of Damage Control has been dealt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.060
with, Bruno breaks the most important news "Ms.&nbsp;
Marvel" has to offer. Before leaving for CalTech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.560
he tells Kamala that she possesses a genetic&nbsp;
mutation, which means her powers don't come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.840
exclusively from the bracelet and Kamala&nbsp;
is actually — dun, dun, dun — a mutant.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.060
Forget Patrick Stewart's cameo on Earth-838&nbsp;
– this is what fans of "X-Men" have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:07.380
waiting for since Disney acquired Fox. Just in&nbsp;
case audiences thought it was a coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:12.434
showrunners added a few bars of the animated&nbsp;
"X-Men" theme music to the scene for good measure.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.320
About a year before "Thor: Love and&nbsp;
Thunder" premiered, fans learned that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.820
Russell Crowe would be joining the MCU as&nbsp;
Zeus. While Zeus is both a fixture of Greek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:25.020
mythology and a character in Marvel Comics,&nbsp;
Crowe is best known for portraying serious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.520
often conflicted characters, like "Maximus"&nbsp;
in Ridley Scott's epic film, "Gladiator."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.620
Given those credentials, many expected Crowe's&nbsp;
Zeus would be a mighty, if not somewhat dour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:40.380
god. Instead, Crowe gave fans a glimpse of Zeus as&nbsp;
they'd never seen him before. A Zeus turned loose.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:45.420
Sporting foppish ringlets, an ill-fitting&nbsp;
breastplate, and making pronouncements in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.980
a ridiculous Greek accent, it was immediately&nbsp;
clear this once mighty "King of the Olympians"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.420
had been entirely transformed by director&nbsp;
Taika Waitit's trademark brand of comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:58.920
relief. Crowe's Zeus prances around with his&nbsp;
toga mini-skirt pinched between his fingers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:04.380
threatens to ban anyone who upstages him before&nbsp;
the annual orgy. He's a riot and an effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:09.840
foil to Thor, embodying an apathetic god who&nbsp;
is everything Gorr, the God Butcher, loathes.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:18.300
"You are Thor, the god of thunder. But, is&nbsp;
not thunder just the sound of lightning?"

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.440
The headlines around "Thor: Love and&nbsp;
Thunder" mostly had to do with Natalie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.340
Portman's return as Jane Foster. Not&nbsp;
only that, she was getting promoted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:30.907
true co-star and would appear alongside&nbsp;
Chris Hemsworth's Thor as Mighty Thor.

00:07:30.907 --> 00:07:31.987
"Jane?"

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.080
When fans speculated about the plot, they&nbsp;
mostly wondered whether she might be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.220
one wielding Mjolnir going forward. Others&nbsp;
wondered whether Christian Bale's Gorr the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:48.840
God Butcher might live to threaten the MCU's&nbsp;
growing cast of gods in future installments.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.660
What no one saw coming was that&nbsp;
"Thor: Love and Thunder" would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.720
end with the God of Thunder becoming&nbsp;
a foster parent to Gorr's daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.940
a little girl named "Love." Thor functionally&nbsp;
adopts the child, but the mystery remains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:05.601
how long he will stay in "dad mode" and what part&nbsp;
Little Love will play in the next MCU phases.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.560
The first-ever Marvel special presentation,&nbsp;
"Werewolf by Night," dropped onto Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.940
around Halloween with minimal fanfare. It&nbsp;
was a test of sorts for one-off stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:19.560
that didn't warrant a feature-length film or a&nbsp;
multi-episode arc. That "Werewolf by Night" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.540
so good was a pleasant surprise. The primarily&nbsp;
black-and-white, low-fi mini-monster movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:30.180
a throwback to horror classics from the 1930s&nbsp;
and '40s like "Frankenstein" and "The Wolfman,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:35.100
but it has a strong voice all its own and an&nbsp;
aesthetic unlike anything else in the MCU.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:40.080
When human monster hunter Jack Russell arrives at&nbsp;
Bloodstone Manor, viewers know that he is destined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.580
to undergo a transformation. This meant that the&nbsp;
best trick "Werewolf by Night" had up its sleeve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:49.140
was the double reveal lurking in the hedge maze.&nbsp;
In a scare worthy of its vintage inspirations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:54.240
a giant arm bursts through a shrubbery and&nbsp;
grabs Jack by the chest. A split second later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.220
we discover it belongs to "Man-Thing," a&nbsp;
popular character from '70s-era Marvel comics.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.840
Further, we learn that Jack isn't hunting&nbsp;
Man-Thing – they're best buds. He's infiltrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.800
this occult occasion at significant risk&nbsp;
to himself to spring his friend before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.360
assembled assassins can torture and kill him.&nbsp;
Man-Thing's design and his sweet relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:17.935
with Werewolf by Night were definitely two&nbsp;
unexpected highlights of the MCU's 2022 line-up.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:23.640
The second special presentation of 2022 was"The&nbsp;
Guardians of the Galaxy Holiday Special," a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:27.120
parody of a Christmas special that was filmed&nbsp;
concurrently with "Guardians of the Galaxy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.380
Vol. 3." This 40-minute musical&nbsp;
mash-up is even more irreverent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.980
than Guardians usually is, and it's&nbsp;
stuffed full of gleeful Easter eggs.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.820
The plot revolves around Drax and&nbsp;
Mantis wanting to get Peter Quill the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.260
best Christmas present ever, his hero,&nbsp;
Kevin Bacon. The actor was referenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.860
in the first film when Star-Lord cited&nbsp;
"Footloose" before his climactic dance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:51.942
battle with Ronan. Bacon doesn't just make a&nbsp;
cameo, either; he plays a significant role.

00:09:54.296 --> 00:09:56.866
"Dude, calm down. We're not gonna hurt you."

00:09:56.968 --> 00:09:58.503
"That's a talking raccoon."

00:09:58.503 --> 00:09:59.163
"I'll kill you."

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:04.020
However, "The Guardians of the Galaxy Holiday&nbsp;
Special" ends with a development that matters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:08.940
much more to the MCU in the long run. Mantis&nbsp;
has been keeping a secret. It turns out she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:13.560
Ego's daughter, which means she's Quill's&nbsp;
half-sister, and she is afraid to tell Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.880
because it might dredge up painful memories&nbsp;
about his mother's death. After the presents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:22.980
have been exchanged and Kevin Bacon heads home,&nbsp;
Mantis decides to tell Peter, and as one would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:26.985
expect in a holiday special, he is thrilled&nbsp;
at the news that he and Mantis are family.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:32.460
After dozens of films, MCU fans know to stick&nbsp;
around for mid and end-credits stingers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:37.080
which often have two purposes. They give&nbsp;
filmmakers one last gag, and more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.580
they tease new storylines and characters. It's a&nbsp;
tradition that goes back to the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:47.940
and 2022 was no exception, with audiences meeting&nbsp;
at least three new additions to the MCU's roster.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.740
First up, audiences finally got to meet&nbsp;
the much-teased Jake Lockley, the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:57.360
violent of Moon Knight's alter-egos. Another&nbsp;
surprise was the appearance of Charlize Theron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.380
as a multiverse traveler who confronts&nbsp;
Doctor Strange in the street. He follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:05.160
her into what appears to be the Dark&nbsp;
Dimension, but not before an ominous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.200
third eye manifests on his forehead. Though she&nbsp;
is only named in the credits, Theron is Clea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.580
Doctor Strange's love interest from the&nbsp;
comics. Next, in "Thor: Love and Thunder,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.960
a miffed Zeus with a gaping hole in his&nbsp;
chest calls upon Hercules to avenge him.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:25.000
Of course, with Bob Iger reinstalled as Disney&nbsp;
CEO and the MCU reassessing its path forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.674
it remains to be seen what role any of&nbsp;
these newcomers will play in the future.

